      /* ---------- story 1 */
      "rl.st.lunar.title": "The Lunar Legacy",
      "rl.st.lunar.tag": "Six landings, twelve moonwalkers and everything they left behind",
      "rl.st.lunar.lead": "Between July 1969 and December 1972 six Apollo crews landed on the Moon. Each time only the ascent stage returned to orbit with two astronauts and their samples. Descent stages, science stations, tools and three Lunar Roving Vehicles stayed on the surface.",
      "rl.st.lunar.c1h": "Launch pads left on the Moon",
      "rl.st.lunar.c1": "The descent stage was the launch platform for the ascent stage, so every Apollo site keeps one: Eagle, Intrepid, Antares, Falcon, Orion and Challenger. A stainless-steel plaque on the ladder of Eagle’s descent stage still marks the first landing at Tranquility Base.",
      "rl.st.lunar.c2h": "Seen again from orbit",
      "rl.st.lunar.c2": "NASA’s Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter has photographed the Apollo sites many times. Low-altitude images released in 2011 showed the Apollo 12, 14 and 17 sites with descent stages, rover tracks and the footpaths of the astronauts.",
      "rl.st.lunar.c3h": "Heritage worth protecting",
      "rl.st.lunar.c3": "The Artemis Accords, established in 2020, include a section on preserving outer space heritage. In the United States the One Small Step to Protect Human Heritage in Space Act became law on 31 December 2020; it ties NASA lunar partners to recommendations that protect the Apollo sites.",
