      "rl.st.rovers.title": "The Silent Rovers",
      "rl.st.rovers.tag": "Two rovers built for 90 sols that kept going for years",
      "rl.st.rovers.lead": "In January 2004 the twin Mars Exploration Rovers landed on opposite sides of Mars: Spirit in Gusev Crater and Opportunity in Meridiani Planum. Each was planned for about 90 Martian days and ran on solar power.",
      "rl.st.rovers.c1h": "Spirit: stuck, but still working",
      "rl.st.rovers.c1": "On 1 May 2009 Spirit became stuck in soft soil beside “Home Plate”. In January 2010 it was turned into a stationary science platform. It fell silent in March 2010, and after more than 1,300 commands NASA ended recovery efforts on 25 May 2011. Spirit had driven 7.73 km.",
      "rl.st.rovers.c2h": "Opportunity: a marathon on Mars",
      "rl.st.rovers.c2": "At its landing site Opportunity found hematite-rich “blueberries” — evidence of a wet past. In 2014 it set the record for the longest drive on another world. A planet-wide dust storm starved its solar panels; the last signal came on 10 June 2018, after 45.16 km. Its final resting place is Perseverance Valley on the rim of Endeavour Crater.",
