      "rl.st.experiments.title": "Forgotten Experiments",
      "rl.st.experiments.tag": "Instruments left on purpose — and some that still answer",
      "rl.st.experiments.lead": "Much of what remains on the Moon and Mars is not wreckage but scientific equipment, placed to keep measuring after the crews and rovers had gone.",
      "rl.st.experiments.c1h": "Stations that listened to the Moon",
      "rl.st.experiments.c1": "Apollo 12, 14, 15, 16 and 17 crews set up ALSEP science stations powered by SNAP-27 radioisotope generators. Their seismometers recorded deep and shallow moonquakes and hundreds of meteoroid impacts a year, revealing the Moon’s layered interior. Mission Control switched the stations off on 30 September 1977.",
      "rl.st.experiments.c2h": "Mirrors that still reflect",
      "rl.st.experiments.c2": "The laser retroreflectors left by Apollo 11, 14 and 15 are still used to measure the Earth–Moon distance. The French-built reflector on the Soviet Lunokhod 1 was lost for decades; after LRO images located the rover in 2010, the Apache Point Observatory team received laser returns from it again.",
      "rl.st.experiments.c3h": "Listening to Mars",
      "rl.st.experiments.c3": "In 2008 Phoenix confirmed water in a heated sample of Martian soil. InSight, NASA’s first mission to explore the deep interior of Mars, placed a seismometer on the ground in 2018; it detected more than 1,300 marsquakes and was the last instrument still powered as dust covered the solar panels.",
      "rl.st.experiments.t1": "Apollo 11 crew leaves a laser retroreflector at Tranquility Base",
